From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:09:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A window is a light

Patch 0030 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The Temple of Time's windows are glass painted on its walls, not openings, so no traced sun can
come through them and the hall below was lit as though they were stone. The user, 2026-10-08:
"the windows in the Temple of Time ... to be actual light sources that cast shadows per window
across the room, and that would allow those to actually accurately behave like a window".

The texture cache now also measures each texture's brightness and contrast as it is queued, and
keeps whether it is a bright lattice: a mean brightness of at least 0.6 and a spread of at least
0.25. The Temple of Time's lattice glass reads 0.71 and 0.33; its stone, as bright, spreads only
about 0.1. Where the program says a place's windows give light (a new RaytracingEnvironment
field, set from the game's scene), each upright, flat draw of a bright lattice at least 60 units
each way becomes a window light: daylight falling into the room through that rectangle, inward
and down at about 53 degrees below level. Inward is the side the eye is on.

The local lights pass lights a surface where the light, followed back up its direction, comes
through the glass, with shadow rays back out the same way, so a pillar between a window and the
floor shadows the floor in the window's own shape, one shadow per window. The edge of the light
softens with the distance it fell. The air's glow leaves windows out. Windows share the eight
places after the game's lights with the beams of patch 0029, and are never merged with each
other or with a beam.

Rung 37 leaves the windows dark as well as the beams.

